/**

@page documentation_exahype2_solvers_aderdg ADER-DG solvers

\tableofcontents

An ADER-DG time step predicts a space-time solution in each cell, projects it
to the faces, solves the face Riemann problems, and corrects the cell solution.

@image html documentation/exahype2/solvers/ADER-DG.png

# Data flow

The predictor uses the cell polynomial to form a space-time approximation. It
writes the time-averaged solution and flux onto the cell's own half of every
face. Once both halves of a face hold their traces, the Riemann solver
produces the numerical flux of the face, which the corrector adds to the cell.

For a linear PDE, the face contribution can be integrated in time before its
spatial projection. This avoids a space-time face representation in the
corrector.

# The time step

The solver family exahype2::dg::ader::Solver runs one time step as:

1. The predictor dispatch over the owned cells writes the traces into the
   state and flux pools of the faces.
2. The halo exchange posts the halves of the exchanged faces. While the
   messages travel, the local level jumps fill their fine faces from their
   coarse face and the Riemann dispatch over the local faces writes the
   numerical flux of both halves.
3. After the wait, the remote level jumps interpolate and the Riemann dispatch
   runs over the remote faces.
4. The level jumps restrict the fluxes of their fine faces onto their coarse
   face, the flux halves of the exchanged faces travel again while the
   corrector dispatch adds the face integrals of the local cells, and the
   remote cells follow after the wait. The corrector reports the wave-speed
   maxima the next time step size reads.

A solver with a subcell limiter (exahype2::dg::limiting::SubcellLimiter)
judges every corrected candidate by its physical admissibility and by the
discrete maximum principle, spreads the troubled status over
`NumberOfSpreadRings` rings of neighbours and recomputes the limited cells
with the finite-volume scheme of the limiter on their subgrids.

# Adaptive interfaces

A hanging face couples cells on different levels. The coarse trace is
interpolated onto the fine faces before the Riemann dispatch, the fine faces
solve their Riemann problems, and the numerical fluxes of the fine faces
restrict onto the coarse face before the corrector of the coarse cell reads
it. No predictor runs twice.

# Coupling two solvers

Two ADER-DG solvers of one project can own complementary parts of the domain
through `restrict_to_cells()`, and `project.couple(first, second)` runs both
with the smaller of their time step sizes; the traces of the faces between
their cells are copied from the partner's face pools, mapped onto the other
solver's face nodes and compute precision. `tests/aderdg-coupling` couples a
double and a single precision solver of the same equation.

*/
